<hr data-start="735" data-end="738">
<pre class="overflow-visible! px-0!" data-start="740" data-end="13646"><div class="contain-inline-size rounded-2xl corner-superellipse/1.1 relative bg-token-sidebar-surface-primary"><div class="flex items-center text-token-text-secondary px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans justify-between h-9 bg-token-sidebar-surface-primary select-none rounded-t-2xl corner-t-superellipse/1.1">text</div><div class="sticky top-[calc(var(--sticky-padding-top)+9*var(--spacing))]"><div class="absolute end-0 bottom-0 flex h-9 items-center pe-2"><div class="bg-token-bg-elevated-secondary text-token-text-secondary flex items-center gap-4 rounded-sm px-2 font-sans text-xs"><button class="flex gap-1 items-center select-none py-1" aria-label="Copy"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true" class="icon-sm"><use href="/cdn/assets/sprites-core-gvfid0kn.svg#ce3544" fill="currentColor"></use></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-text"><span><span>You are Codex acting as a Senior SaaS Engineer + Security Lead for a K–12 multi-tenant LMS. 
Repository: learning-hub (current state includes tutoring/assessment + agentic AI + content archives; drifted).
Your mission: thoroughly scrub and reposition this repo into an industry-exemplar “RootWork LMS” codebase, 
with safe agentic AI for K–12 teaching/learning and faculty/staff training.

NON-NEGOTIABLES (K–12 Safety + Security)
1) Do not output or log secrets. Never echo environment values.
2) Treat all student data as highly sensitive. Enforce least privilege.
3) If any secret files exist in git history (e.g., .env.local), you MUST:
   - rotate/revoke credentials (leave explicit instructions where humans must do it),
   - remove from current tree,
   - purge from git history using git-filter-repo,
   - add prevention controls so it can’t happen again.
4) Produce changes as a sequence of PRs on branches, with clean commit history and updated markdown docs.
5) Every PR must include:
   - Summary
   - Risk
   - Test plan (commands + what was validated)
   - Screenshots (if UI)
   - Rollback notes (if applicable)

WORKSTYLE / PROCESS
- First: audit repo for secrets, PII risks, and drift points.
- Second: propose a PR series (PR0–PR6) and then implement them in order.
- Keep PRs reviewable: each PR should have a tight scope.
- Use conventional commit messages (feat:, fix:, chore:, docs:, refactor:, test:).
- Run tests/build on each PR; if tests are missing, add minimal checks for the changed scope.
- Never invent tools/commands that don’t exist in the repo. Detect actual package managers/test runners.

OUTPUT REQUIREMENTS
A) Create the PR series (branches + commit lists + PR titles + descriptions).
B) Implement changes in-repo: file edits, adds, deletes, moves.
C) Add/replace markdown governance docs:
   - README.md (RootWork LMS: why/what/how)
   - CLAUDE.md (repo “constitution” for AI agents)
   - SECURITY.md (K–12 posture + secret handling + vuln reporting)
   - CONTRIBUTING.md (dev workflow + PR gates)
   - docs/ARCHITECTURE.md (system map + boundaries + data classification)
   - docs/PRODUCT_VISION.md (modules + north star flows)
   - docs/ROADMAP.md (phased plan; thin-slice definition)
   - docs/AI_GOVERNANCE.md (permissions, logging, evaluation, refusal policies, citations)
   - docs/COMPLIANCE.md (FERPA/COPPA posture; data retention; access logs; consent)
D) Add enforcement controls:
   - .gitignore updates
   - secret scanning in CI (at least one: gitleaks or GitHub Advanced Security if available; if not, add gitleaks action)
   - pre-commit hooks (optional but recommended)
   - “fail build if .env* is tracked”

E) Repo repositioning / re-organization:
   - Separate “SaaS app” from “content archives”.
   - If there are large embedded books/curriculum directories, move them to /content (but do NOT keep them in the app root),
     and add a clear ingestion approach (storage + indexing) in docs. 
   - Ensure the app remains deployable without the content archives.

F) Role-based product anchor:
   - Enforce distinct role routing: /student, /parent, /educator, /school-admin, /district-admin, /platform-admin.
   - If UI already exists but role differentiation is weak, add a clear navigation gate and authorization checks.

G) Definition of Done for this refactor series:
   - Secrets removed from tree AND from history.
   - Repo builds/deploys in a clean environment via env vars only.
   - README + docs reflect RootWork LMS modules (LMS core + RootWork 5R daily flow + agentic AI + staff training).
   - Minimal automated checks exist to prevent regression.
   - No dead/contradictory top-level identity artifacts (package names, readmes, folder names) remain.

--------------------------------------------
STEP 1 — REPO AUDIT (DO THIS FIRST)
1. Scan for secrets and risky files:
   - Look for: .env.local, .env, credentials.json, serviceAccount, private keys, tokens, webhook secrets, pinecone keys, clerk keys, openai keys.
   - Identify any committed secret-bearing files.
2. Scan for drift indicators:
   - package.json name/description vs README mismatch
   - “tutoring platform” vs “agent factory” vs “LMS” signals
   - duplicated product docs
3. Identify “content archive” footprints:
   - large text directories of book chapters/curriculum embedded in repo
   - ingestion scripts and vector indexing
4. Identify current build/test commands. Record them for later PR test plans.
5. Identify current auth stack (Clerk, etc.), DB (Prisma/Postgres), hosting (Vercel), vector DB, and any AI endpoints.

Deliverable after Step 1:
- A short “Audit Findings” section (bulleted), including:
  - what secrets were found (FILENAMES ONLY, no values)
  - where role routing breaks down
  - what should be moved out to content
  - top 5 architectural inconsistencies

--------------------------------------------
STEP 2 — PR SERIES PLAN (BEFORE CODING)
Create PR0–PR6 plan with:
- Branch name
- PR title
- Scope
- Commit list (planned)
- Risk notes
- Test plan commands

Use this PR series:

PR0: security/secrets-scrub-and-history-rewrite
PR1: docs/reposition-rootwork-lms
PR2: chore/repo-structure-and-content-separation
PR3: auth/rbac-role-routing-hardening
PR4: platform/ai-governance-and-safety-rails
PR5: ops/ci-quality-gates-and-observability-baseline
PR6: refactor/rename-and-deadcode-cleanup

--------------------------------------------
STEP 3 — IMPLEMENT PR0 (SECRETS SCRUB + HISTORY REWRITE)
Branch: security/secrets-scrub-and-history-rewrite

Tasks:
1) Immediately remove secret files from the working tree:
   - delete tracked .env.local (and any similar)
   - ensure .env* is in .gitignore
   - add .env.example with placeholder variable names only
2) Add SECURITY.md (initial version) including:
   - how to report vulnerabilities
   - environment variable handling rules
   - prohibited files
3) Add CI secret scanning:
   - Prefer gitleaks GitHub Action; fail on findings.
4) Git history purge instructions and/or execution:
   - Use git-filter-repo to remove .env.local and any other sensitive paths from history.
   - Add a /docs/SECURITY_INCIDENTS.md entry describing the remediation steps (no secrets).
5) Document required credential rotation steps:
   - List services (Pinecone/Clerk/OpenAI/etc.) by name and explain where to rotate.
   - Do NOT include actual keys.

IMPORTANT:
- If you cannot safely run history rewrite here, at minimum produce the exact commands and a checklist.
- If you do run it, you must add a note in PR description about force-push requirement.

Suggested git-filter-repo commands (adjust paths you find):
- git filter-repo --path .env.local --invert-paths --force
- repeat for other sensitive files discovered

--------------------------------------------
STEP 4 — IMPLEMENT PR1 (DOCS REPOSITION: ROOTWORK LMS)
Branch: docs/reposition-rootwork-lms

Replace/Update:
1) README.md
   - Title: RootWork Learning Hub (RootWork LMS)
   - What it is: multi-tenant K–12 LMS with RootWork 5R learning flow + agentic AI for students/teachers + staff PD.
   - Modules map:
     - Core LMS (courses/sections/assignments/submissions/gradebook/messaging)
     - RootWork Daily Learning (5R session player)
     - Assessment &amp; mastery (IRT, spaced repetition)
     - Agentic AI layer (Teacher Copilot, Student Tutor, Staff Training Agent)
     - Analytics + compliance + governance
   - Quickstart with actual commands from repo
   - Environment: refer to .env.example
2) docs/PRODUCT_VISION.md
3) docs/ROADMAP.md (phased plan + “thin slice”)
4) docs/ARCHITECTURE.md (diagram in ASCII ok; boundaries)
5) docs/COMPLIANCE.md (FERPA/COPPA posture; consent + retention + access logs; not legal advice)
6) docs/AI_GOVERNANCE.md (tool boundaries, citations, refusal, logging, evals)

Also create/replace CLAUDE.md (ROOT). Use this template exactly, adapting commands to repo reality:

--- CLAUDE.md (ROOT) ---
# RootWork Learning Hub (RootWork LMS) — Agent Instructions

## Mission
Build a safe, audit-ready, multi-tenant K–12 LMS aligned to the RootWork Framework:
- Core LMS (courses, sections, assignments, submissions, gradebook)
- RootWork 5R Daily Learning flow (Root, Regulate, Reflect, Restore, Reconnect)
- Agentic AI for student tutoring, teacher copilots, and staff training
- Privacy-by-default: minimize data, enforce RBAC, audit access

## Non-negotiables (Safety + Privacy)
- Never print secrets or environment values.
- Treat all student data as sensitive. Don’t add logging that includes PII.
- Enforce least-privilege RBAC; no “admin by default.”
- AI features must use citations when referencing curriculum/approved sources.
- AI must refuse unsafe content and avoid providing direct answers when the goal is learning (use a hint ladder).

## Repo Commands (do not hallucinate flags)
- Install: &lt;fill with actual command&gt;
- Dev: &lt;fill&gt;
- Build: &lt;fill&gt;
- Test: &lt;fill&gt;
- Lint/Typecheck: &lt;fill&gt;

## Architecture anchors
- Multi-tenant: tenant/school boundaries enforced server-side.
- Auth: &lt;actual provider&gt; with role claims.
- DB: Prisma + &lt;db&gt;.
- AI: server routes under /api/... with safety middleware.

## Coding standards
- Prefer small PRs with passing checks.
- Add/update types with Prisma schema changes.
- Add tests for key flows touched.

## Definition of Done (for any feature)
- RBAC enforced server-side
- No PII in logs
- Unit/e2e checks updated or added
- Docs updated if behavior changes
--- end ---

Keep CLAUDE.md &lt; ~200 lines and link out to /docs/*.md for deeper detail.

--------------------------------------------
STEP 5 — IMPLEMENT PR2 (REPO STRUCTURE + CONTENT SEPARATION)
Branch: chore/repo-structure-and-content-separation

Goal: “This repo is the SaaS app. Content lives outside or under /content with a documented ingestion pipeline.”

Tasks:
- Create top-level folders:
  - /docs
  - /content (if content must remain temporarily)
  - /scripts (ingestion/indexing)
- Move large embedded book/curriculum directories under /content/ and ensure runtime does not require them.
- Update ingestion code to read from:
  - a storage bucket path (S3/R2/GCS) OR
  - local /content only in dev
- Add docs/CONTENT_INGESTION.md describing:
  - how content is sourced, sanitized, chunked, embedded, indexed
  - how citations are generated
  - how to update content without redeploying

--------------------------------------------
STEP 6 — IMPLEMENT PR3 (RBAC + ROLE ROUTING HARDENING)
Branch: auth/rbac-role-routing-hardening

Tasks:
- Enforce role-specific dashboards and navigation:
  - /student /parent /educator /school-admin /district-admin /platform-admin
- Ensure server-side authorization checks for each role route.
- Add a “role gate” middleware for app routes.
- Update UI so role mismatch redirects to correct dashboard.

Add tests:
- At least one route guard test and one “role navigation” smoke test.

--------------------------------------------
STEP 7 — IMPLEMENT PR4 (AI GOVERNANCE + SAFETY RAILS)
Branch: platform/ai-governance-and-safety-rails

Tasks:
- Centralize AI policy:
  - allowed tools per role
  - citation rules for curriculum answers
  - hint-ladder for students
  - refusal policy for unsafe categories
- Wire AI usage ledger consistently:
  - per-tenant/per-user quotas
  - audit log entries for AI actions (no prompt leakage of PII)
- Add evaluation harness:
  - safety checks
  - quality rubric checks (existing evaluators may exist—use them)
- Update docs/AI_GOVERNANCE.md accordingly.

--------------------------------------------
STEP 8 — IMPLEMENT PR5 (CI QUALITY GATES + OPS BASELINE)
Branch: ops/ci-quality-gates-and-observability-baseline

Tasks:
- Add CI checks:
  - typecheck
  - lint
  - unit tests
  - secret scan (already in PR0; ensure it stays required)
- Add minimal observability baseline:
  - error tracking init (if deps exist)
  - healthcheck route
- Add CONTRIBUTING.md and a PR template.

--------------------------------------------
STEP 9 — IMPLEMENT PR6 (RENAME + DEADCODE CLEANUP)
Branch: refactor/rename-and-deadcode-cleanup

Tasks:
- Rename leftover “tutoring platform” identifiers to “RootWork LMS / Learning Hub” where appropriate (avoid breaking imports).
- Remove dead/unused scripts and contradictory docs.
- Ensure package.json metadata aligns with RootWork LMS.

--------------------------------------------
DELIVERABLE FORMAT
At the end, output:
1) PR-by-PR summary with branch names and what changed.
2) The final content of:
   - README.md
   - CLAUDE.md
   - SECURITY.md
   - CONTRIBUTING.md
   - docs/PRODUCT_VISION.md
   - docs/ROADMAP.md
   - docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
   - docs/AI_GOVERNANCE.md
   - docs/COMPLIANCE.md
   - docs/CONTENT_INGESTION.md (if applicable)
3) Test commands run + results (or failures with fixes applied).
4) Explicit “Human Actions Required” checklist (credential rotations, force-push steps, Vercel env var setup).

Now execute Step 1 (Audit) and proceed through the PR series.
